## 4.5 Issue a book

Implement a real interactive issue-book workflow.

The librarian should be able to:
1. Select or search for an existing member.
2. See the member's identity and membership status.
3. View their currently issued books and overdue obligations.
4. Search for an available book copy.
5. Select the copy to issue.
6. Review the issue date and due date.
7. Confirm the transaction.
8. Receive a clear success or validation message.

Enforce the following:
- Only eligible members can borrow books.
- Only available copies can be issued.
- A copy cannot be issued to two members simultaneously.
- Respect configured issue limits.
- Respect configured overdue restrictions, if enabled.
- Prevent invalid or duplicate transactions.
- Update the member's current loans and the copy's availability immediately.
- Save a transaction history record.
- Generate a receipt or printable transaction summary if the existing frontend supports it.

Use sensible default issue and due dates, but allow authorized users to change them when the policy permits.

Make issue failures understandable. For example, explain if no copy is available or the member has reached the borrowing limit.

## 4.6 Return books

Implement a complete return workflow.

The librarian should be able to:
- Search by member, book title, accession number, or transaction ID.
- View active loans and their due dates.
- Select a specific issued copy.
- Review overdue days and applicable fine calculations.
- Confirm the return.
- Record the condition of the returned book.
- Add a note for damaged or missing items where appropriate.

On a successful return:
- Close the active loan.
- Update the copy's availability or condition.
- Record the actual return date.
- Calculate any applicable fine using the configured policy.
- Update the member's current loan list.
- Record the transaction in history.
- Display a clear confirmation.

Prevent returning the same active loan twice.

A returned damaged copy should not automatically become available if it needs repair or has been withdrawn.

## 4.7 Renewals

Allow eligible active loans to be renewed.

Check:
- Whether the loan is still active.
- Whether the member is allowed to renew.
- Whether a renewal limit exists.
- Whether the book has a reservation that prevents renewal.
- Whether the maximum due-date extension has been reached, if configured.

Update the due date and renewal history correctly.

Display why a renewal is unavailable instead of silently failing.

## 4.8 Reservations and holds

Create a reservation workflow for books that are unavailable or whose copies are currently issued.

Support:
- Create a reservation for an eligible member.
- Display reservation position or priority if the existing rules support it.
- View pending reservations.
- Cancel a reservation.
- Mark a copy ready for collection when it becomes available.
- Record collection or expiry of a reservation.
- Prevent duplicate active reservations by the same member for the same title unless the configured policy allows them.

If reservation notifications are simulated, clearly label them as demo notifications.

Do not claim that an email or SMS has been sent unless an actual integration exists.

## 4.9 Fines and adjustments

Support a configurable fine policy if the project architecture allows it.

Potential policy settings:
- Fine per overdue day.
- Grace period.
- Maximum fine.
- Exemptions or approved waivers.
- Rules for lost or damaged books.

Display the calculation clearly:
- Due date.
- Actual return date or current date for an overdue loan.
- Overdue days.
- Daily fine rate.
- Applied grace period or cap.
- Total assessed fine.
- Payments or adjustments recorded.
- Remaining balance.

Use consistent date arithmetic and avoid charging a returned loan repeatedly.

Allow only appropriately authorized users to record fine payments, adjustments, or waivers.

Record who performed a financial adjustment when the existing audit model supports it. Do not represent a fine as paid merely because the librarian opened or edited the record.

If fine payment is only simulated, make that explicit.

## 4.10 Lost, damaged, and missing books

Provide workflows for:
- Reporting a lost book.
- Recording damage.
- Marking a copy missing.
- Recording repair or replacement.
- Withdrawing a copy from circulation.
- Reviewing any approved replacement charge.
- Maintaining an incident or condition history.

Do not delete a book-copy record just because it is lost or damaged.

Keep the distinction between a lost copy, a damaged copy, a missing copy, and an available copy.

Update available-copy counts correctly.

## 4.11 Inventory and stock verification

Provide:
